# surrounding condition itself changed), so `git apply` correctly refuses
# both forward and reverse application. The other two hunks in this same
# patch (llama-model-loader.h struct change, llama-model.cpp
# init_mappings/buf_map changes) apply with a pure line-number offset (no
# content conflict) because nothing else in this pinned commit touched
# those regions.
#
# This rebased hunk 3 keeps the qwen38 intent (choose the mmap-vs-upload
# path per BUFFER via the new `llama_buf_info::is_mmap` flag introduced by
# hunks 1-2 of the same patch, instead of blanket `use_mmap`) while
# preserving the independently-added `lazy.has(cur)` disjunct verbatim, so
# lazy tensors keep working exactly as the pinned commit already has them
# working: `tensor_uses_mmap` replaces the `use_mmap` half of the OR,
# `lazy.has(cur)` is untouched.
#
# What was verified (see this task's integration report for the exact
# commands): (1) source commit and patch-repo commit SHA both match the
# pin via `git rev-parse HEAD` after a fresh, isolated clone; (2) this
# rebased patch applies with `git apply --check` cleanly (rc=0, no
# fuzz/offset) directly on top of pinned-commit + first patch; (3) after
# applying, no leftover `bufs.at(idx)`/`bufs.count(idx)` call anywhere in
# `src/llama-model-loader.cpp` still assumes the old bare
# `ggml_backend_buffer_t` value type instead of `llama_buf_info` (grep
# swept the whole load_all_data function, not just the touched hunks).
#
# What was NOT verified (do not assume it): this candidate was not
# compiled (no ROCm/HIP toolchain was invoked in this pass), so no
# guarantee is made about template/overload resolution, ABI, or actual
# runtime memory behavior (the actual "Qwen3.8 Flash Next keeps its sparse
